1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,758
Here are our two actual CAD parts.

2
00:00:02,758 --> 00:00:05,812
Orange is the cooling body; teal is the cover.

3
00:00:05,812 --> 00:00:06,874
Both are copper.

4
00:00:06,874 --> 00:00:10,194
The cover approaches the channel side of the body.

5
00:00:10,194 --> 00:00:17,033
We will inspect the mating features, show the seal locations, align the parts, and bring them together.

6
00:00:18,633 --> 00:00:22,241
First, turn the cover over so both mating faces are visible.

7
00:00:22,241 --> 00:00:26,270
The body carries the water channel and two circular O ring grooves.

8
00:00:26,270 --> 00:00:31,081
The cover has two water holes and a short pocket for the proposed divider strip.

9
00:00:31,081 --> 00:00:36,433
This flipped position is only for inspection; the cover must rotate back before assembly.

10
00:00:38,033 --> 00:00:41,563
The green traces mark the inner and outer O ring positions.

11
00:00:41,563 --> 00:00:45,093
They separate the wet annular region from its surroundings.

12
00:00:45,093 --> 00:00:48,503
The gold trace marks the proposed internal divider strip.

13
00:00:48,503 --> 00:00:51,495
Its job is to reduce the inlet to outlet shortcut.

14
00:00:51,495 --> 00:00:56,700
These are seal location graphics, not finished seal models or a compression simulation.

15
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:03,949
Now rotate the cover into its installation orientation, with the fitting bosses pointing rearward.

16
00:01:03,949 --> 00:01:08,503
The guide lines connect each cover water hole to the corresponding channel end.

17
00:01:08,503 --> 00:01:12,595
The six clearance holes must align with the body's six receiving holes.

18
00:01:12,595 --> 00:01:15,996
The divider pocket must also face the solid channel bridge.

19
00:01:15,996 --> 00:01:19,167
Matching the circular bolt pattern alone is not enough.

20
00:01:20,767 --> 00:01:25,461
Slide the cover straight toward the body without twisting the seals out of position.

21
00:01:25,461 --> 00:01:29,262
At zero gap, the flat metal lands meet at their shared mating plane.

22
00:01:29,262 --> 00:01:37,198
The circular grooves remain one point five millimetres deep for the proposed two millimetre rings, giving twenty five percent nominal squeeze.

23
00:01:37,198 --> 00:01:41,167
Actual seal compression and cover stiffness still require verification.

24
00:01:42,767 --> 00:01:49,751
Six screws enter from the rear, pass through the cover clearance holes, and engage the intended M three threads in the body.

25
00:01:49,751 --> 00:01:54,087
The highlighted circles show their locations; actual screws are not included.

26
00:01:54,087 --> 00:01:58,706
Tightening should be developed in opposing steps with a validated load and torque.

27
00:01:58,706 --> 00:02:02,367
The cover screws are separate from the front target clamp screws.

28
00:02:03,967 --> 00:02:08,641
With the cover hidden for this internal view, follow the intended water route.

29
00:02:08,641 --> 00:02:16,311
Water passes through one boss and cover hole, travels around the C shaped channel, then returns through the other hole and boss.

30
00:02:16,311 --> 00:02:19,427
The bridge and proposed strip discourage a shortcut.

31
00:02:19,427 --> 00:02:22,783
The moving dots are explanatory, not a fluid simulation.

32
00:02:22,783 --> 00:02:25,300
Bypass and pressure loss must be measured.

33
00:02:26,900 --> 00:02:28,368
This is the fitted pair.

34
00:02:28,368 --> 00:02:34,668
The flat front face supports the future target, while the cover remains attached during target changes.

35
00:02:34,668 --> 00:02:41,335
The plates together are ten millimetres thick, with the bosses projecting another eight millimetres rearward.

36
00:02:41,335 --> 00:02:45,066
This is a nominal CAD fit, not a pressure qualified assembly.

37
00:02:45,066 --> 00:02:51,367
Fittings, seals, fasteners, and the surrounding vacuum and electrical insulation still need completion.
